# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Nehemiah'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'history'
SUBTITLE = 'The Wall Rebuilt, the Law Read, and the Covenant Renewed'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Nehemiah begins with the prayer of the cupbearer to the king of Persia and follows his return to Jerusalem. It records the rebuilding of the wall in fifty and two days, the reading of the book of the law, the confession of the people, the sure covenant and its oath, the dedication of the wall, and Nehemiah’s final reforms (Neh. 1:5; 2:17–18; 6:15; 8:1, 8; 9:17, 38; 10:29; 12:43; 13:14).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'This book belongs entirely to the Prophecy Program, where a remnant of Israel rebuilds Jerusalem and renews the covenant under the law of Moses (Neh. 8:1; 10:29).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Nehemiah', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — Nehemiah’s Prayer and Commission (Chs. 1–2)'), ('p', 'Nehemiah prays to the LORD God of heaven and tells the people, “Let us rise up and build” (Neh. 1:5, 9; 2:17–18, 20).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — The Wall Built (Chs. 3–7)'), ('p', 'The wall is built and finished in fifty and two days (Neh. 4:6; 6:15).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — The Law Read and the Covenant Renewed (Chs. 8–10)'), ('p', 'Ezra reads the book of the law, they give the sense, the people confess, and they make a sure covenant (Neh. 8:1, 8, 12; 9:5–6, 17, 32, 38; 10:29).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — Dedication and Reform (Chs. 11–13)'), ('p', 'The wall is dedicated with great joy, and Nehemiah prays, “Remember me, O my God, for good” (Neh. 12:43; 13:14, 31).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Nehemiah', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Great and Terrible God</b> — “O LORD God of heaven, the great and terrible God, that keepeth covenant and mercy for them that love him and observe his commandments” (Neh. 1:5).', '<b>The Place He Chose</b> — “Will I gather them from thence, and will bring them unto the place that I have chosen to set my name there” (Neh. 1:9).', '<b>The Wall Finished</b> — “So the wall was finished in the twenty and fifth day of the month Elul, in fifty and two days” (Neh. 6:15).', '<b>The Law Read and Understood</b> — “They read in the book in the law of God distinctly, and gave the sense, and caused them to understand the reading” (Neh. 8:8).', '<b>A Sure Covenant</b> — “We make a sure covenant, and write it” (Neh. 9:38).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Nehemiah', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how Nehemiah points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Restored City</b> — Jerusalem’s wall is rebuilt by a returned remnant (Neh. 6:15), reported as written within Israel’s program.', '<b>The Book of the Law</b> — The people ask for the book of the law of Moses, which the LORD had commanded to Israel (Neh. 8:1).', '<b>The Covenant Oath</b> — They enter into an oath to walk in God’s law, which was given by Moses (Neh. 10:29).'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Nehemiah', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Nehemiah speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Nehemiah operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Nehemiah in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Nehemiah reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['the rebuilt wall and city (Neh. 2:17; 6:15)', 'the book of the law of Moses (Neh. 8:1)', 'the sure covenant and oath (Neh. 9:38; 10:29)', 'the place the LORD chose for His name (Neh. 1:9)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Nehemiah that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Nehemiah contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['The Place Chosen — “the place that I have chosen to set my name there.” (Neh. 1:9).', 'Rise Up and Build — “Let us rise up and build.” (Neh. 2:18).', 'Wall Finished — “in fifty and two days.” (Neh. 6:15).', 'The Book of the Law of Moses — “the book of the law of Moses, which the LORD had commanded to Israel.” (Neh. 8:1).', 'A Sure Covenant — “we make a sure covenant, and write it.” (Neh. 9:38).', 'The Oath — “to walk in God’s law, which was given by Moses the servant of God.” (Neh. 10:29).', 'The Wall Dedicated — “God had made them rejoice with great joy.” (Neh. 12:43).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Nehemiah Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Nehemiah and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Nehemiah teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Nehemiah', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['The Place Chosen', '“the place that I have chosen to set my name there.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Neh. 1:9'], ['Rise Up and Build', '“Let us rise up and build.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Neh. 2:18'], ['Wall Finished', '“in fifty and two days.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Neh. 6:15'], ['The Book of the Law of Moses', '“the book of the law of Moses, which the LORD had commanded to Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Neh. 8:1'], ['A Sure Covenant', '“we make a sure covenant, and write it.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Neh. 9:38'], ['The Oath', '“to walk in God’s law, which was given by Moses the servant of God.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Neh. 10:29'], ['The Wall Dedicated', '“God had made them rejoice with great joy.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Neh. 12:43']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Nehemiah that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Nehemiah teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['God Keeps Covenant', '“the great and terrible God, that keepeth covenant and mercy for them that love him.”', 'Neh. 1:5'], ['The LORD Alone', '“Thou, even thou, art LORD alone; thou hast made heaven, the heaven of heavens.”', 'Neh. 9:6'], ['Ready to Pardon', '“thou art a God ready to pardon, gracious and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness.”', 'Neh. 9:17'], ['Understanding the Word', '“they had understood the words that were declared unto them.”', 'Neh. 8:12'], ['Remember Me for Good', '“Remember me, O my God, for good.”', 'Neh. 13:31']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Nehemiah without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Nehemiah in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Pray with confidence in His covenant mercy</b> (Neh. 1:5).', '<b>Confess the LORD alone as Creator</b> (Neh. 9:6).', '<b>Trust that He is ready to pardon</b> (Neh. 9:17).', '<b>Read the Word until it is understood</b> (Neh. 8:8, 12).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Nehemiah', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Nehemiah in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['Prayer and Commission (Chs. 1–2) — Rise up and build. <i>(Neh. 1:5, 9; 2:17–18)</i>', 'The Wall Built (Chs. 3–7) — Fifty and two days. <i>(Neh. 4:6; 6:15)</i>', 'The Law Read (Chs. 8–10) — The sure covenant. <i>(Neh. 8:1, 8, 12; 9:6, 17, 38; 10:29)</i>', 'Dedication and Reform (Chs. 11–13) — Remember me for good. <i>(Neh. 12:43; 13:14, 31)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Nehemiah, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward prayer, perseverance, and humility without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Nehemiah simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Nehemiah stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]

